          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The concept of
            %em BV-operator
            has two different but (somewhat subtly) related meanings:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                In
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
                (discussed
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#InPerturbativeQuantumFieldTheory"} below
                %em BV-operator
                or
                %em BV-Laplacian
                (
                %a{:href => "#BatalinVilkovisky81"}> Batalin-Vilkovisky 81
                ) is essentially the difference between the action of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV+differential"} BV differential
                on the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+observables"} algebra of observables
                before and after
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free field theory
                around which the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantization
                is considered; it is a quantum correction
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi ℏ
                      %msub
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi BV
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \hbar \Delta_{BV}
                (of order of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℏ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV+differential"} BV differential
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi s
                          %mi BV
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mi S
                        %mo ′
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo .
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s_{BV} = \{-S',-.\}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mi S
                          %mo ′
                          %mo ,
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                          %mo )
                        %mn 2
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left(\{-S',-\}\right)^2 = 0
                for the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-differential"} BV-differential
                to be a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential"} differential
                is called the “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/master+equation"}> master equation
                ” in
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+theory"} BV-BRST theory
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mi S
                          %mo ′
                          %mo ,
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                          %mo +
                          %mi i
                          %mi ℏ
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo )
                        %mn 2
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left( \{-S',-\} + i \hbar \Delta\right)^2 = 0
                is called the
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+master+equation"} quantum master equation
            %li
              %p
                In
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+algebra"} higher algebra
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-algebra"} BV-algebra
                with the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+homology"} chain homology
                of a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/little+k-cubes+operad"} E2-algebra
                %em BV-operator
                corresponds to the operation of rotating a little disk around.
          %p
            For the relation between the two see
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+BV+and+BD"} relation between BV and BD
          %h2#InPerturbativeQuantumFieldTheory In perturbative quantum field theory
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            the BV-operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mi ℏ
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \hbar \Delta
            may be understood intuitively as reflecting the contribution of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gaussian+measure"} Gaussian measure
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free field theory
            around which the perturbative quantization takes place. This intuition may be made precise for finite-dimensional toy path integrals. This we discuss in:
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#ForFiniteDimensionalToyPathIntegrals"} In finite-dimensional toy path integrals
          %p
            In the rigorous construction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+field+theory"} relativistic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            via
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+AQFT"} perturbative AQFT
            there is a rigorous incarnation of the BV-operator (
            %a{:href => "#FredenhagenRejzner11b"}> Fredenhagen-Rejzner 11b, section 2
            ,
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#Rejzner11"} Rejzner 11, section 5.1.2
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+S-matrix"} perturbative S-matrix
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time+ordered+products"} time ordered products
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+polynomial+observables"} regular polynomial observables
            is the difference between the classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-differential"} BV-differential
            and its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjugation"} conjugation
            into the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+products"} time-ordered products
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ForGaugeFixedFreeLagrangianFieldTheoryBVOperator"}
            below). This we discuss in
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#InCausalPerturbationTheory"} In causal perturbation theory
          %h3#ForFiniteDimensionalToyPathIntegrals For finite-dimensional toy path integrals
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+number"} finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+manifold"} closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} oriented
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration+of+differential+forms"} integration of differential forms
            of top degree over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            may be identified with sending such differential forms to their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+cohomology"} de Rham cohomology
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            This may be slightly reformulated: Fixing a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/volume+form"} volume form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            it induces by contraction with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+fields"} vector fields
            degreewise a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+isomorphism"} linear isomorphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi Γ
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi n
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo *
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mi Γ
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mo ∧
                    %mrow
                      %mi dim
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo −
                      %mi n
                  %mi T
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mu \;\colon\;
                  \\Gamma_X(\wedge^n T^\ast X)
                  \\overset{\simeq}{\longrightarrow}
                  \\Gamma_X(\wedge^{dim(X)-n} T X)
          %p
            between the spaces of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+n-forms"} differential n-forms
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and the space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multivector+fields"} multivector fields
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            of degree
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mi dim
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n-dim(X)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+differential"} de Rham differential
            induces a differential on
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multivector+fields"} multivector fields
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi BV
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi μ
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi dR
                  %mo ∘
                  %msup
                    %mi μ
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Delta_{BV}
                  \\;\coloneqq\;
                  \\mu \circ d_{dR} \circ \mu^{-1}
          %p
            This
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi BV
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_{BV}
            is the
            %em BV-operator
            in this simple situation.
          %p
            The above statement about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integration
            now translates into saying that for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration+of+differential+forms"} integration of differential forms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∫
                  %mi f
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int f \mu
            may be identified with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+homology"} chain homology
            of the BV-operator
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi BV
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_{BV}
          %p
            If one thinks of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            as a space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+configurations"} field configurations
            and of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo =
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mi ℏ
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f = \exp(i \hbar S)
            as an exponentiated
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∫
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mi ℏ
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int \exp(i \hbar S) \mu
            as the finite-dimensional toy version of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
          %p
            While this is in general not defined in the actual non-finite dimensional situations in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+theory"} field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+homology"} chain homology
            of a BV-operator does make sense whenever an appropriate kind of differential is given. One may then try to axiomatize which chain complexes qualify as BV-complex and try to interpret their chain homology as computing perturbative
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integrals"} path integrals
          %p
            For more on this perspective see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+formalism"} BV-BRST formalism
            the section
            %em
              %a{:href => "BV-BRST+formalism#HomologicalIntegration"} Quantum BV as homological (path-)integration
          %h3#InCausalPerturbationTheory In causal perturbation theory
          %p
            For more context for the following see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A+first+idea+of+quantum+field+theory"} A first idea of quantum field theory
            the chapter
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "A+first+idea+of+quantum+field+theory#FreeQuantumFields"} Free quantum fields
          %h4#background Background
          %p Recall the following context
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E,\mathbf{L})
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theory"} Lagrangian field theory
            admitting a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing"} gauge fixing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi L
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi L
                  %msub
                    %mo ′
                    %mi BRST
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{L}' - \mathbf{L}'_{BRST}
            be its BV-BRST
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+density"} Lagrangian density
            after
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing"} gauge fixing
            (
            %a{:href => "A+first+idea+of+quantum+field+theory#GaugeFixingLagrangianDensity"}> this def.
            ), so that the gauge-fixed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+BRST+complex"} local BV-BRST differential
            is given by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+antibracket"} local antibracket
            as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo ′
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mrow
